1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:10,440
- Do you travel with a laptop? - My laptop has been dead for about six years. I don't know where the charger is. - We can help you with - That.
ノートパソコン、旅に持っていく？ 僕のパソコン、6年くらい死んでる。充電器がどこかも分からない。それなら手伝えるよ。それ。

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:34,360
I know. We met for the first time in Rome on a small street. - Yes. - You were playing in Rome that week with the smile. Yeah.
知ってる。初めて会ったの、ローマの細い道だった。うん。その週、The Smileでローマに出てた。うん。

3
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:42,640
So I'd just been talking about... I was going to that show. Was it before the show? It was before the show. Yeah.
だからちょうど話してたんだ。あのショーに行くって。ショーの前？ 前だった。うん。

4
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:48,720
And a friend of mine had asked me if we'd ever met and I had said that we hadn't.
友達に、会ったことある？って聞かれて、ない、って言ってた。

5
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:55,520
So I was walking down the street and I noticed you because you were wearing a hat that said acid on it. And I thought, "Oh, that's a cool hat."
道を歩いてたら君に気づいた。アシッドって帽子をかぶってたから。いい帽子だな、って。

6
00:00:55,520 --> 00:01:10,900
And I was like, "That guy's wearing a cool hat." And then I was like, "Oh, it's you." And then I had Moonshape Pool playing in my headphones and I stopped it and was like, "Oh, hello."
いい帽子の人だな、って。それから、あ、君だ、って。ヘッドフォンで『A Moon Shaped Pool』がかかってて、止めて、やあ、って。

7
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:19,280
- Mr. York. - Mr. York. - Hello. I was impressed. - And you said, "Oh, hi." And then - - No, I said, "What the fuck?"
ヨークさん。ヨークさん。こんにちは。感心したよ。君は「やあ」って。いや、僕は「なんだよ」って言った。

8
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:24,760
- Yeah. And - Then - Are you doing standing in the street? - And then I came to the show and then we met kind of properly at the show.
うん。それで、道に立って何してるの？ それからショーに行って、ショーでちゃんと会った。

9
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:47,420
- Yeah. I didn't know you were living in Rome a lot. I had no idea. So I was double confused.
うん。ローマにそんなに住んでるなんて知らなかった。全然。だから二重に混乱した。

10
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:48,820
- Yeah, it was really like, I think for me, through that and then the show, it always been, you to me had always been that person I was kind of scared of meeting.
うん、本当に。僕には、あのこととショーを通して、君はずっと、会うのがちょっと怖い人だった。

11
00:01:48,820 --> 00:02:05,100
- Oh yeah, I've got a whole list. - Yeah, totally. And I remember feeling so light afterwards, I think of just, oh, people and they're kind and they're nice.
ああ、僕にもリストがある。うん、まったく。あとですごく軽くなったのを覚えてる。人って優しくて、いい人なんだ、って。

12
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:13,620
And it was very nice. - I was also impressed that you were just walking down the street. I was like, where's this security?
すごくよかった。君がただ道を歩いてるのも感心した。警備はどこ、って。

13
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:17,900
Because I mean, I know I can do it. I didn't know you could do it. Is that a choice?
僕はできるって分かってる。君ができるとは知らなかった。それ、選んでるの？

14
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:33,180
- In the early days when I was in the band and stuff, so much of the hotel experience felt very locked in.
バンドの初期とか、ホテルはすごく閉じ込められてた。

15
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:46,500
It felt like there was this like, oh, it's not safe for me to be out in some way. Yeah, that - Makes sense.
外に出るのは、なんか安全じゃない、みたいな感じ。うん、それは分かる。

16
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:49,520
- And then I remember I had a girlfriend who was very outgoing and she was coming to London and I was kind of like, oh
それから、すごく外向的な彼女がロンドンに来ることになって、

17
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:58,780
if we just sit in my house for three days, she's going to think I'm nuts. So we kind of walked everywhere and it was this realization for me of like, oh, it's fine to be outside.
家に3日いたら、僕がおかしいと思われる、って。だからどこでも歩いた。外にいていいんだ、って気づいた。

18
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:12,500
It kind of broke that thing. And then now it's changed my life in such a big way. It's like how you stay connected to the world and the experience.
それが壊れた。今は人生がすごく変わった。世界や経験と、つながり続ける方法みたいに。

19
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:27,600
And I read something about walking being the artist's kind of perspective because you're at ground level and you see things that you just don't see if you're driving - Everywhere.
歩くのはアーティストの視点だ、って読んだ。地面の高さにいるから、車だと見えないものが見える。どこでも。

20
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:41,680
It's also a good way to write. I wrote Wolf at the Door. I was going through a really bad moment and I walked for like two and a half days, rain, didn't matter.
書くのにもいい。『Wolf at the Door』はそう書いた。すごく悪い時期で、2日半くらい歩いた。雨でも関係なく。

21
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:51,880
A notebook in my hand and just wrote all and I felt so much better at the end of it. But also it's not just the walking, it's the walking past people.
ノートを手に、全部書いた。終わりにはすごく楽になった。でも歩くだけじゃない。人の横を通り過ぎること。

22
00:03:51,880 --> 00:04:04,820
The simple thing of people watching, playing that game of imagining as someone walks by where they're going, what they're doing, all that stuff brings you out of yourself
人を見る、あの単純なこと。通り過ぎる人がどこへ行くか、何をしてるか想像する。それが自分の外に出してくれる。

23
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:12,499
brings you out of your troubles. I mean, without it, See, it's really easy to get lost without being able to do that.
悩みの外へ。それがないと、本当に迷子になりやすい。

24
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:25,340
I guess I had a similar thing, but it happened for you very young, happened for me later. But I remember when we were on tour with REM and Michael Stipe figuring out that...
似たことはあった。君はすごく若いときに起きて、僕はあとだった。REMのツアーで、マイケル・スタイプが気づいた。

25
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:33,040
I mean, well, it was kind of obvious because when we played, I would just hide in the dressing room before them and never went out anywhere or whatever.
Obviousだった。僕らが出るとき、僕は彼らの前、楽屋に隠れて、どこにも出なかった。

26
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:42,920
So he started dragging me out and he gave me this trick. He said, "If you don't care, they don't care. If you're not looking, they're not looking."
だから引っ張り出されて、技をもらった。気にしなきゃ、向こうも気にしない。見てなきゃ、見られない、って。

27
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:55,200
And it works. I remember being in Stockholm going to some really busy club and he said, "Try it." And it works and nobody bothered us. So there are moments when you will feel vulnerable.
効くんだ。ストックホルムの、すごく混んだクラブで「やってみろ」って。効いて、誰も構ってこなかった。傷つきやすい瞬間はある。

28
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:07,540
There are moments when shit will happen or the sun will be printing your picture one too many times and you'll not want it or something. And I've had that.
ひどいことが起きる瞬間もある。写真を刷られすぎて、もう嫌になるとか。僕もあった。

29
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:16,040
It's not an easy decision to make, but it's better than the alternative.
簡単な決断じゃない。でも、もう一方よりはいい。

30
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:17,060
The alternative, I know people who got death threats and then wouldn't leave the house and had security patrolling their house to hold all that sort of stuff.
もう一方は、殺害予告を受けて家から出られなくなって、警備が家を巡回する。そういうの全部。

31
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:28,620
You don't want that. I think if you have something that's very successful, there's a lot of pressure to repeat that, which I'm sure you'll probably feel or you do feel sometimes.
それは嫌だ。すごく当たったものがあると、それを繰り返せ、って圧力が大きい。君も感じるだろうし、ときどき感じてると思う。

32
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:41,660
It doesn't sort of go anywhere because that's like working to commission. You know what I mean? It's like we like those. Let's paint that - Again. - Yeah.
それはどこにも行かない。注文仕事みたいだから。分かる？ あれが好きだった。もう一回それを描いて。うん。

33
00:05:37,940 --> 00:05:48,000
Could you do that? I remember some movies, some being drawn in to watch some clip of the movie, "We'd really like you to do something like this."
できる？ 映画の一部を見せられて、こういうのをやってほしい、って引き込まれるのを覚えてる。

34
00:05:48,000 --> 00:06:00,340
And I was like, "Use that." That sort of mentality of like, "We want your name at the top, but we'd like you to repeat yourself, but
それを使え、って。名前は一番上に欲しい。でも自分を繰り返してほしい。

35
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:08,240
we don't want to ask you to repeat yourself because we know it's rude. So we're kind of thinking about it's commercial art form, so therefore you're always going to get people asking you to do that."
繰り返せとは言いたくない。失礼だと分かってるから。商業のアートだから、いつもそう頼まれる、って考えてる。

36
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:33,220
- It's an interesting cycle, I think, because you make something that I think connects with people because they find something in it that you've captured something about the human experience, whether it's a
面白い循環だと思う。人とつながるものを作る。人がその中に、人間の経験の何かを見つけるから。

37
00:06:31,020 --> 00:06:43,900
vulnerability or a sensitivity that comes with being human. And then I think any kind of success around that thing makes it take on this life of its own.
弱さとか、人間でいることの繊細さ。それから成功が、それに勝手な命を与える。

38
00:06:43,900 --> 00:07:02,780
Then everything around you kind of makes your experience incredibly not human and then moving - - Yes, artificial. - Yeah.
周りの全部が、経験をものすごく人間じゃなくする。それから動いていく。うん、作り物。うん。

39
00:07:02,780 --> 00:07:04,220
And then it feels like the kind of goal is to how do you shut that out when you're making stuff so that you can still be making stuff about the human experience.
ものを作るとき、それをどう遮って、まだ人間の経験について作れるか、が目標みたいになる。

40
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:18,620
I've always thought if I was to make music about being in a studio and touring and all of the kind of shiny things that come with that and how wonderful that is -
スタジオやツアーや、ついてくるキラキラについて音楽を作ったら、っていつも思ってた。それがどれだけ素晴らしいか。

41
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:53,319
It's - Shiny for you, mate. It's - Not shiny very well. It's definitely not interesting to me, so it can't possibly be interesting to anyone else.
君にはキラキラだよ。あんまりキラキラしてない。僕には全然面白くない。だから他の人にも面白いはずがない。

42
00:07:54,760 --> 00:07:57,440
What I love about your work, for example, is for so many people who look at you in this way of in this kind of godlike status in terms of a creative - And
君の仕事が好きなのは、たくさんの人が、創造の神みたいに君を見てるのに。そして

43
00:07:54,760 --> 00:08:11,680
- An artist to still be someone who kind of looks at, oh, this is the gritty part of life.
アーティストなのに、まだ、これが人生のざらついた部分だ、って見てる人でいること。

44
00:08:12,619 --> 00:08:15,279
I think that's what's so impressive about so much of your work is that for people to revere you in such a way - And to - Still be looking at what's under the
それがすごいところだと思う。あんなに崇められて、まだ爪の下を見てる。

45
00:08:12,619 --> 00:08:29,140
fingernails rather than... - All I can think is I think the reason for that was it's as much having to learn quite a difficult lesson.
爪の下を。それより上じゃなくて。思えるのは、かなり難しい教訓を学ばなきゃいけなかったからだ、ってこと。

46
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:32,580
And if you have to do lots of interviews, for example, if you end up having to explain your work in a particular way, when people are like...
インタビューをたくさんやらなきゃ、仕事を決まった説明の仕方で説明することになる。人が…

47
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:45,900
You can get picked apart and what you've done can get picked apart in a way, which is what you mean by artificial, I think really, in a way that's not necessary.
ばらされる。やったことが、ある意味ばらされる。それが君の言う作り物だと思う。必要ない形で。

48
00:08:45,900 --> 00:09:01,060
I mean, it used to be a lot worse. Now I guess it's not as bad, but around kiddo amnesia, before that, I really struggled.
昔の方がずっと悪かった。今はそこまでじゃない。でもKid Aのあたり、その前は本当に苦しかった。

49
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:05,420
Every time I had to try to do something new, I was explaining it to myself while I was doing it, literally. It's like a form of madness and I couldn't get out of it.
新しいことをやるたびに、やりながら自分に説明してた。文字通り。狂気の一種で、抜けられなかった。

50
00:09:05,420 --> 00:09:24,700
The experience of trying to make music, even the experience of being in the studio or listening back to stuff became this really weird hollow thing because I felt I needed to justify it
音楽を作ること、スタジオにいること、聴き返すことまで、変な空洞になった。正当化しなきゃ、って感じたから。

51
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:29,400
somehow, but not justify it in a sort of apologize, justify it in a kind of intellectual way. And the reason for that was I needed to get out of it.
謝る正当化じゃなくて、頭で正当化する。理由は、そこから出なきゃいけなかったから。

52
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:39,160
I needed to just get out of the studio and I needed to just walk. A lot of that was down to the fact that nobody cares, man.
スタジオを出て、ただ歩かなきゃ。多くは、誰も気にしてない、ってことだった。

53
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:53,080
Nobody cares that it's difficult. And so if you're worried, if you're bummed out that it's difficult, fucking stop, go and do something else.
誰も、難しいことなんて気にしない。難しいって落ち込んでるなら、やめろ。他のことをしろ。

54
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:56,320
Also, later on as you have kids and all this kind of thing, the choice to carry on doing this kind of work, you have to enjoy it.
子どもができたりすると、この仕事を続けるには、楽しさが要る。

55
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:09,160
And the enjoyment comes from having to start again from scratch every time you start again, whatever you learn, you've got to unlearn or all this kind of nonsense.
楽しさは、始めるたびにゼロからやり直すことから来る。学んだことは、学び直さなきゃいけない。そういう話。

56
00:10:10,220 --> 00:10:22,680
Also, songwriting is a very odd... I mean, going to the Ivan Novellos thing, I mean, I don't hang out with songwriters, but apparently I am one. And you realize that actually songwriting is quite a naive thing.
曲作りはすごく変で。アイヴァー・ノヴェロの話に行くと、ソングライターとはつるまない。でもどうやら僕はそうらしい。曲作りは、かなり無邪気なことだと分かる。

57
00:10:23,560 --> 00:10:36,400
It's like you can't be savvy about it. You can work really hard on it, but the good stuff just happens and you don't know why.
抜け目なくはできない。すごく働ける。でもいいものは、ただ起きる。なぜか分からない。

58
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:38,180
And you've got to be honest with yourself that you don't know why, and you've got to carry on being honest with yourself that you don't know why.
なぜか分からない、って自分に正直でいなきゃ。分からないまま、正直でい続けなきゃ。

59
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:48,040
And when stuff forms that you like, you just grab onto it and you don't let it go because the stuff you like is you don't know why you like it.
好きな形ができたら、掴んで離さない。好きなものは、なぜ好きか分からないから。

60
00:10:48,040 --> 00:10:57,880
Why would Pyramid Song... I mean, it's three chords over and over again and the same lyric. How does that make sense?
なぜ『Pyramid Song』が。コード3つを繰り返して、歌詞も同じ。なんで成立するんだ？

61
00:10:55,580 --> 00:11:20,180
That kind of thing. But you like it, you like it. You're not bothered about if anyone else likes it.
そういうこと。でも好きなんだ。好きなんだ。他の人が好きかどうかは気にしない。

62
00:11:20,180 --> 00:11:22,120
- I've always wondered if part of that is why the idea of imposter syndrome that can come with commercial success exists is I think if you write in that way
ずっと不思議だった。商業的な成功についてくる、偽物みたいな感じの一部は、そういう書き方だからじゃないか、って。

63
00:11:20,180 --> 00:11:32,580
there's a part of you that kind of acknowledges... It almost feels like you're not responsible for it.
一部は認めてる。自分が責任を持ってないみたいに感じる。

64
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:35,280
So then if people are like, "Oh, you've made this thing and that means that you're this," there's something in there that feels like, "Oh, I can't take credit for it.
人が「これを作った、だから君はこれだ」って言うと、功績は受け取れない、って感じがある。

65
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:59,780
It just kind of happened to me."
ただ、僕に起きただけ、って。

66
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,680
I think I've always found the things that I end up liking the most or have been the most special things to me are the ones where you kind of write it and then
いちばん好きになるもの、いちばん特別なものは、書いてから、

67
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,680
as you're listening back or reading lyrics back, you're kind of learning something about yourself of you're realizing that you've said something rather than I'm going to sit down, I'm going to say this.
聴き返したり歌詞を読み返したりして、自分について何かを学んでるものだ。これを言おう、と座って言うんじゃなくて、言ってしまったことに気づく。

68
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:11,920
Yeah. - And you might not realize and you might record it and track it and everything and not realize until you've mastered it. Yeah. And then you suddenly hear, "Oh shit, that's what I - Meant."
うん。気づかないこともある。録って、全部やって、マスタリングするまで気づかない。うん。それから「くそ、これが言いたかったことだ」って。

69
00:12:10,920 --> 00:12:26,040
Yeah. - Yeah, that's right. I think, but at the same time, it's not not you because you spend... It's not divine intervention or anything like that. It's like you got to show up.
うん。うん、その通り。でも同時に、君じゃないわけじゃない。時間をかけてるから。神が降りてくるとかじゃない。出てこなきゃいけない。

70
00:12:26,040 --> 00:12:37,500
In order to find these things, you have to show up. Much like with any job, you develop skills at what you do. If you're a cameraman, you develop skills, you develop a relationship with your camera.
見つけるには、出てこなきゃ。どんな仕事でも同じ。腕がつく。カメラマンなら、カメラとの関係ができる。

71
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:52,700
If you are a songwriter, you develop a relationship with whatever the hell that is.
ソングライターなら、それが何であれ、それとの関係ができる。

72
00:12:52,700 --> 00:12:56,800
You have to learn to accept the months of imposter syndrome where you're like, "I don't have anything to justify all this amazing equipment in my studio. I can't find anything I like.
偽物みたいな感じの何ヶ月かを受け入れなきゃ。スタジオのすごい機材を正当化するものが何もない。好きなものが見つからない。

73
00:12:56,800 --> 00:13:08,640
That's okay." And then you'll pick up your notebook or whatever and there'll be one line in it that sets you off. But the point is you showed up because it's really easy not to show up.
それでいい。ノートを拾うと、一行があって、それが火をつける。要点は、出てきたこと。出てこないのは本当に簡単だから。

74
00:13:08,640 --> 00:13:16,240
It's really easy to go, "Oh, I'll get to that one. Oh, I know what I'm doing. It's fine." Because it's the small acts that end up being the big acts.
あとでやる、何をしてるか分かってる、大丈夫、は簡単。小さいことが、大きいことになるから。

75
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:33,580
It's really easy to get into this kind of mentality that everything is a performance where it's not everything is searching, in fact, and searching for stuff that speaks to you, not anybody else.
全部がパフォーマンスだ、って考えに入りやすい。実際は全部が探すこと。自分に話しかけるものを探す。他の誰かじゃない。

76
00:13:33,580 --> 00:13:47,620
- Someone wants to describe it to me as kind of like surfing where you can practice getting up on the board and the waves are kind of going and sometimes you'll be practicing,
誰かがサーフィンに例えた。ボードに立つ練習はできる。波は来てる。ときどき練習してても、

77
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:59,300
but there's no waves. And then if a wave comes but you haven't practiced enough, you're not going to be able to surf it. That's right.
波がない。波が来ても、練習が足りないと乗れない。その通り。

78
00:13:59,300 --> 00:14:00,680
And - Then - When they kind of happen, it's like the combination of you've practiced being able to capture it and it's something is happening at the right time.
それが起きるときは、掴む練習をしてきて、正しいタイミングで何かが起きてる。

79
00:14:00,680 --> 00:14:07,160
- Yeah, you're ready when it's... Yeah. - And that's when it's like - - Or if I'm surfing, you're not ready ever or you're on the rock scope.
うん、準備ができてるとき。うん。それが、みたいな。僕がサーフィンしてたら、いつまでも準備できないか、岩の上にいる。

80
00:14:07,160 --> 00:14:20,380
Just - Like being open. And I think I used to feel really... I've had a big shift away from... I think I used to feel really labor focused about art in general.
ただ開いてること。昔はアートを、労働で測ってた。大きな変化があった。

81
