00:28:13,900 --> 00:28:22,280
- Right. - I think if there was any - I'm - Surprised - Kind of scariest version to me. - The last record is very much, it sounds like it was developed in the studio.
そう。何かがあったら。驚いてる。僕にとっていちばん怖い版。前のレコードは、すごく、スタジオで育った音だ。

164
00:28:22,280 --> 00:28:32,360
- With an intention. - It doesn't sound like it comes from voice notes. - That one was less from voice notes. - Now you see. - Yeah.
意図を持って。ボイスメモから来た音じゃない。あれはボイスメモからは少なかった。ほら。うん。

165
00:28:27,420 --> 00:28:42,780
I mean, there was a few. It's kind of like moments in this. There's the kind of orchestral thing or the piano songs. There's a couple of much - More - Straightforward songs.
いくつかはあった。この中の瞬間みたいに。オーケストラっぽいもの、ピアノの曲。もっとまっすぐな曲がいくつか。

166
00:28:42,780 --> 00:28:51,820
And then yeah, there's probably three or four kind of moments that I think are the most down the middle like there. - So the rest are worked up in the studio?
それから、いちばん真ん中、って瞬間が3つか4つ。残りはスタジオで作り上げた？

167
00:28:51,820 --> 00:29:04,740
- Yeah, it was mostly done in the studio this time. - Written in the studio. - A lot of it, yeah.
うん、今回はほとんどスタジオ。スタジオで書いた。多くは、うん。

168
00:29:04,740 --> 00:29:12,220
I think it was just where I felt like I was at in my life and music that I was experiencing. I want to melt the parts of my life together.
人生と、聴いてた音楽の、その場所にいたからだと思う。人生の部分を溶かしたかった。

169
00:29:12,220 --> 00:29:22,880
I'd started going out dancing a lot more and I really didn't want it to be like, "Oh, I'm listening to this music. This is kind of where I'm at."
踊りに出ることが増えてた。これが聴いてる音楽、今はここ、ってにしたくなかった。

170
00:29:22,880 --> 00:29:28,940
And then I go in the studio and be like, "Well, I have to make this sort of music instead." I think it was - Really bad. I know that feeling.
スタジオに入って、代わりにこういう音楽を作らなきゃ、って。本当に嫌だった。その感じ、知ってる。

171
00:29:28,940 --> 00:29:39,560
- Yeah. I think it was really like... - I know that feeling very well. - "I've just come from this place. I want that beat to keep going, or I want...
うん。本当に。その感じ、よく知ってる。さっきあの場所から来た。あのビートを続けたい。あるいは。

172
00:29:41,860 --> 00:29:54,440
It was a lot about, for me, I think what I was experiencing at that time in my life. It was like I want to be on stage feeling in the middle of it like this.
多くは、そのときの人生で感じてたことだった。ステージの真ん中で、こう感じていたい、って。

173
00:29:54,440 --> 00:30:01,580
And that was kind of the thing that - I - Think you did that. - Informed the record the - Most.
それが、いちばんレコードを形にした。君、それやってたと思う。

174
00:30:01,580 --> 00:30:08,340
I mean, that was my favorite That's kind of my favorite part of the show where you literally do that. There was a generosity to that, which I though, yeah, that's really cool.
あれ、僕のお気に入りだった。ショーで、文字通りそれをしてるところが好き。気前があった。本当にクールだ、って。

175
00:30:08,340 --> 00:30:17,340
And that is clearly someone who's discovering this thing and wants to share it in a real... So honesty of it was very cool. I was really into that.
明らかに、これを見つけて、本当に分かち合いたい人。その正直さがすごくよかった。本当にハマってた。

176
00:30:17,340 --> 00:30:29,240
I mean, it's also very difficult to take that kind of music into those places. It's much easier to just make yourself totally deaf DJing in a club in Berlin and thinking that's okay.
ああいう音楽を、ああいう場所に持っていくのはすごく難しい。ベルリンのクラブでDJして、耳が完全にいかれて、それでいい、の方がずっと簡単。

177
00:30:29,240 --> 00:30:40,140
And it is okay. Dance music has got a lot to offer and it's not just one thing.
それでいい。ダンスミュージックには渡せるものがたくさんある。一つじゃない。

178
00:30:41,080 --> 00:30:54,800
We went through this phase when Radiohead were on tour, we had Caribou supporting us, and now I was DJing with him afterwards. It's crazy.
レディオヘッドのツアーで、カリブーが前座で、そのあと彼とDJしてた時期があった。狂ってる。

179
00:30:54,800 --> 00:30:55,920
Finishing the show and then starting up at a club at one or two or even three in the morning and then it's a day off maybe the next day if you're lucky or
ショーを終えて、クラブを1時、2時、3時に始めて、次の日が休みならラッキー。

180
00:30:54,800 --> 00:31:05,560
not. I know that feeling. I want this. Yeah. The reason you brought that, I was asking you like, yeah, but the new one you didn't write off voice notes, right?
そうじゃないときもある。その感じ、知ってる。これが欲しい。うん。それを出したのは、新しいのはボイスメモでは書いてないよね、って聞いてたから。

181
00:31:05,560 --> 00:31:26,820
So that means then you're changing the way you write. - I mean, I've always written a lot in the studio.
だから、書き方が変わってる。スタジオでは、いつもたくさん書いてきた。

182
00:31:26,820 --> 00:31:29,700
I think I tended to, what it used to be, I think was a lot of voice notes and little pieces of things or it would be a line or something and then I'd
昔は、ボイスメモや小さい断片、一行があって、

183
00:31:26,820 --> 00:31:38,800
start things and then take them in and finish them there. And this was the first time it was like, oh, I'd done something at home and I'm like, oh, I think this is the song.
始めて、持っていって、そこで仕上げてた。今回は初めて、家でやって、これが曲だと思う、ってなった。

184
00:31:35,740 --> 00:31:42,900
Okay. - Because now you have a studio at home. Do you work in it or you work with someone?
分かった。今は家にスタジオがある。そこで働く？ 誰かと？

185
00:31:42,900 --> 00:31:48,960
- I work with - Someone - There, yeah. - I mean, I've only just got to the point now where I can turn my own studio and know where everything is.
誰かと、そこで。うん。自分のスタジオの電源を入れて、全部の場所が分かるようになったのは、やっと今。

186
00:31:48,960 --> 00:32:04,300
It's taken me a long time. But once you have a place like that and yeah, it changes how you think about writing anyway because you can just endlessly mess around and then just grab one piece.
長くかかった。ああいう場所があると、書き方の考えが変わる。無限にいじれて、一つの断片を掴める。

187
00:32:04,300 --> 00:32:12,300
That's it. That's what makes me want to sing that bit there. Fuck the rest of it. I'm just going to use that bit. No different than jamming around on an acoustic guitar.
それだ。あそこで歌いたくなる。残りはどうでもいい。あの断片だけ使う。アコギでジャムるのと変わらない。

188
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:31,961
It just takes up some hard disc space, that's all. But that's why I travel with a laptop.
ハードディスクを少し使うだけ。だからノートパソコンを持って旅する。

189
00:32:31,961 --> 00:32:33,321
There's upsides to it and downsides to it, to be honest, because it means you've got everything at your fingertips so you can fiddle endlessly, get really excited about a synth sound and then
正直、いい面と悪い面がある。全部が手元にあるから、無限にいじれる。シンセの音に興奮して、

190
00:32:31,961 --> 00:32:36,001
take it home and show it to Sam and he's like, "Okay, well there's a real synth over there. Why don't you go and use that?"
家に持ってサムに見せると、そっちに本物のシンセがある。あれを使えよ、って。

191
00:32:36,001 --> 00:32:47,381
Laptops have that kind of bad habits, obsessive tendencies, which is not good, but the upside is you have all this kind of tech, you're taking everything out.
ノートパソコンには悪い癖がある。執着。それはよくない。いい面は、この機材が全部あって、全部を外に出せる。

192
00:32:47,381 --> 00:33:02,721
I don't know. When we're doing Kiday, Nigel had a Pro Tools, the first Pro Tools set up because we were having to record strings. We couldn't get them on to the tape and all this sort of stuff.
Kid Aのとき、ナイジェルがPro Toolsを持ってた。最初のやつ。ストリングスを録らなきゃいけなくて、テープに入らなかった。

193
00:33:02,721 --> 00:33:15,361
And I was like, "I need that because it's always a bit of a computer head." So when I saw that in the studio, I'm like, I really, I just wanted to learn it immediately.
あれが要る。僕はちょっとコンピュータ頭だから。スタジオで見て、すぐに学びたかった。

194
00:33:15,361 --> 00:33:26,561
And that really changed how I though about music straight away. Suddenly I have a multi-track in my bag. I was going to ask you, where's home?
音楽の考え方が、すぐに変わった。急に、バッグに多重録音がある。家はどこ？って聞こうとしてた。

195
00:33:26,561 --> 00:33:38,321
- Home? - Do you have more than one? - I think when I'm in London feels the most calm. And I'm not from here. - Where's your stuff?
家？ 一つより多い？ ロンドンにいるときがいちばん落ち着く。ここ出身じゃない。荷物はどこ？

196
00:33:34,221 --> 00:33:53,701
Where's your important stuff? London. - Yeah, London. Yeah, because I've lived in my house for 12 years or something. That's the place where I feel like I really feel like been so many...
大事なものはどこ？ ロンドン。うん、ロンドン。家に12年くらい住んでる。そこが、本当に感じる場所。

197
00:33:54,761 --> 00:34:06,961
I've been through so many versions of myself even in the same space in there that it's the most I feel like I really grew up in this house in a big way.
同じ空間で、自分のいろんな版を通過した。この家で、大きく育った感じがする。

198
00:34:06,961 --> 00:34:19,401
I remember moving in, it was right after my 19th birthday or something. It's been through a lot of changes and things, but it's kind of the place where I'm...
引っ越したのは、19歳の誕生日のすぐあとだった。変化はたくさんあった。でもそこが、僕が。

199
00:34:19,401 --> 00:34:26,541
It's like in my kitchen there is the most like, oh, this is where I live. Right. Do - You cook?
キッチンがいちばん、ここが住んでる場所だ。そう。料理はする？

200
00:34:26,541 --> 00:34:32,801
I - Do. Yeah. - You're probably pretty good, aren't you? All - Right. - I'm fucking terrible. - To stay on you for a second, if I may.
する。うん。たぶん上手だろ？ まあまあ。僕はひどく下手だ。もう少し君の話でいいか。

201
00:34:33,641 --> 00:34:47,762
I was curious doing this interview, for example, or the Ivas thing, for example, I think everyone communicated almost like a surprise that you were willing to be celebrated in some way.
このインタビューや、アイヴァーのことが気になった。祝われることに君が乗る、って、みんながほとんど驚いてた。

202
00:34:48,702 --> 00:35:16,322
And I am curious about how your relationship with that has changed over time in terms of as someone who's so innovative
それとの関係が、時間でどう変わったか気になる。こんなに新しいことをする人として、

203
00:35:14,422 --> 00:35:51,622
how much of it has always been a need to reject any sort of nostalgia to assure yourself that you're moving forward.
前に進んでるって自分に言うために、昔を拒む必要が、どれだけずっとあったか。

204
00:35:51,622 --> 00:35:54,062
To me, it feels like maybe just being in a happier place to be able to realize that you can do both
僕には、もっと楽な場所にいるから、両方できるって気づけるみたいに感じる。

205
00:35:51,622 --> 00:35:54,062
you can still innovate - And - Also allow people to celebrate and for you to celebrate things that you've done whether it be the Ivas or touring and playing a deep selection from your catalog
まだ新しくできて、人も祝えて、自分も祝える。アイヴァーでも、ツアーで昔の曲を深く選んで弾くことでも。

206
00:35:51,622 --> 00:35:54,062
to be able to play those songs and not feel like we're living in the past right now and just be like, "We made something really beautiful I'm going to play it and that's beautiful.
あの曲を弾いて、今は過去に生きてる、って感じなくて。本当に美しいものを作った。弾く。それも美しい。

207
00:35:54,062 --> 00:36:11,502
And also I cannot also still be moving forward."
そして、まだ前にも進める。

208
00:36:11,502 --> 00:36:14,402
- You're right that I'm in a slightly better place nowadays, but I'd be lying that if I didn't say that something like getting a Iva Novello fellowship was not deeply confusing for me.
今は少しいい場所にいる、は正しい。でもアイヴァー・ノヴェロのフェローみたいなものは、深く混乱した、って言わなきゃ嘘になる。

209
00:36:14,402 --> 00:36:28,402
I admire a lot of the people that they've given those awards to. So that's really kind of why I said, "Well, yeah, I would do that because some of these people are my heroes."
賞をもらってきた人の多くを尊敬してる。だから、やるよ、って言った。何人かはヒーローだから。

210
00:36:28,402 --> 00:36:45,082
But I have a very, very strange relationship. God, this is really hard to explain. I have a very strange relationship to that element of my work. The - Navel gazing?
でもすごく変な関係がある。説明するのが本当に難しい。仕事のその部分と、すごく変な関係。自分のへそを見てる？

211
00:36:45,082 --> 00:37:01,903
- No, just - - The kind of exterior. - Yeah, because I find it hard to believe in it, Which is...
いや、ただ。外側みたいな。うん。それを信じるのが難しいから。

212
00:37:03,123 --> 00:37:11,103
I think the reason you talk about this moving forward thing is that the moving forward thing is that's what I believe in.
前に進む話をするのは、前に進むことが、僕が信じてることだからだと思う。

213
00:37:11,103 --> 00:37:39,123
That's easy to believe in because that's new and I don't have to explain it and I don't know what I'm doing, so I'm happy.
それは信じやすい。新しいし、説明しなくていい。何をしてるか分からない。だから楽だ。

214
00:37:40,643 --> 00:37:45,243
When the light is put on you and it's like, "You did this," I'm like, "Yeah, I did, but I can't tell you why, and maybe someone else can, but
ライトが当たって、君がこれをやった、ってなると、うん、やった。でもなぜかは言えない。他の誰かなら言えるかも。

215
00:37:40,643 --> 00:37:45,243
I often feel in these kind of occasions, I even did after the Radiohead tour, even though it was in the middle of it, it was really fun
こういう場では、よくそう感じる。レディオヘッドのツアーのあとも。途中だったのに本当に楽しかった。

216
00:37:40,643 --> 00:37:53,283
my response to it afterwards was like To crash, massively crash in terms of self-confidence. It took me months to... Well, it took me starting on my own record again two months later to go," Okay, I'm good now.
そのあと、自信が大きく崩れた。何ヶ月も。2ヶ月後に自分のレコードを始め直して、やっと、大丈夫、今はいい。

217
00:37:53,283 --> 00:38:05,563
I'm fine. "Because yeah, I just have a really odd relationship with it and I've just grown used to that. To be honest, your speech that you made, his speech was great.
大丈夫。それと本当に変な関係があって、それに慣れただけ。正直、君のスピーチは最高だった。

218
00:38:05,563 --> 00:38:16,323
Number one, because you came with a notebook and read it out the notebook, and number two, I was like," Yeah, man. "Just because it was really honest and personal and I could relate to it.
一つ、ノートを持ってきて、ノートから読んだから。二つ、うん、いいぞ、って。本当に正直で、自分の話で、共感できた。

219
00:38:16,323 --> 00:38:37,443
Whereas when it sort of feels like a business and it feels like you're part of a business and the cameras are running, all that kind of stuff
ビジネスみたいに感じて、その一部で、カメラが回ってる、みたいなとき、

220
00:38:33,843 --> 00:38:37,443
I got this legacy feeling about all of it, which I can't really explain and I probably should figure it out at some point, but I'm happy moving in and out of it.
全部に、遺産みたいな感じがする。説明できない。いつか分かった方がいい。でも出入りできて、それでいい。

221
00:38:37,443 --> 00:39:08,963
- Yeah, something I'm curious about. I wonder if it's something also that maybe happens when you have children and then keep creating, because
気になることがある。子どもができて、まだ作り続けるときに起きることかもしれない。

222
00:39:07,283 --> 00:39:08,963
obviously the idea of that is like you make something that continues to exist and it's not like you have children and then think I have to keep innovating so I'm done with them.
作ったものが残り続ける、って考え。子どもができて、新しくしなきゃだから、もう終わり、とは思わない。

223
00:39:08,963 --> 00:39:19,384
It's almost like both a connection to the things that you do in your life and have done in your life. - Yeah, it's really important that they all integrate.
やってること、やってきたことへの、両方のつながりみたい。うん、全部が一つになるのが本当に大事。

224
00:39:19,384 --> 00:39:34,600
- And can keep informing who - You - Are, but also allowing to celebrate the things that you've done as part of you're really proud of these things and people are proud of
それが、君が誰かを知らせ続けられる。やってきたことを祝うことも許す。本当に誇ってて、人も誇ってて、

225
00:39:32,844 --> 00:39:46,084
those things and it becomes a different - - Well, you probably saw Agnes and Noah.
それが別のものになる。アグネスとノアを見ただろう。

226
00:39:46,084 --> 00:39:48,204
They were very emotional about it and Diana, my wife was as well, and my friends, I do it for them because if they're emotional about it, I'm like, okay, that's why I'm here.
二人はすごく感情的だった。妻のダジャナも。友達も。彼らのためにやってる。彼らがそうなら、だからここにいる、って。

227
00:39:48,204 --> 00:40:05,084
Because for me, I feel kind of numb about it. It doesn't make sense to me.
僕自身は、なんか感覚がない。僕には意味をなさない。

228
00:40:05,084 --> 00:40:07,604
Agnes and Noah, having grown up with their dad doing this, one of the things I'm most proud of is some of the attitude that I have has rubbed off them as they grow
アグネスとノアは、父がこれをやる中で育った。いちばん誇ってるのは、僕の態度の一部が、育つうちにうつったこと。

229
00:40:05,084 --> 00:40:22,324
up. I can see they have that kind of attitude to what they're doing, quite fearless about being creative and quite open and not embarrassed.
やってることに、あの態度がある。作ることにかなり恐れがなくて、開いてて、恥ずかしくない。

230
00:40:22,324 --> 00:40:31,604
I mean, obviously when your kid's a teenager, they're like, "Oh God, my father's blah, blah, blah." But now it's really nice. It's a good thing. I feel super lucky about that because it wasn't easy for a long time.
ティーンのときは、父さんがどうこう、だ。今は本当にいい。いいことだ。すごくラッキーだ。長いこと、簡単じゃなかったから。

231
00:40:31,604 --> 00:40:58,424
- There's kind of an option of when the thing that comes with you make something that resonates with people and then that noise and all of that and your experience becomes so existential
人と響くものを作ると、騒音がついてきて、経験がすごく「何のためだ」になる、って道がある。

232
00:40:56,704 --> 00:41:03,884
of what is it for and what is this for and generally what is what we all doing here and all of that stuff. There's a version of that is kind of like, "Oh, it's all meaningless, so why bother?"
何のため、これは何のため、僕らはここで何をしてるんだ。一つの方は、全部無意味だから、なんでやる。

233
00:41:03,884 --> 00:41:11,504
And then on the other side of that is it's all kind of meaningless so you can go and enjoy it a bit. - When you say meaningless, what do you mean?
反対側は、全部なんか無意味だから、少し楽しめる。無意味って、何を言ってる？

234
00:41:11,504 --> 00:41:22,204
Which element meaningless? - I think general, any sort of existentialism towards meaning of life, like any sort - Of - In the work?
どの部分が？ 人生の意味みたいなもの全般。仕事の中の？

235
00:41:22,204 --> 00:41:52,125
- Yeah. I think in writing about the human experience and what it means to be a human in the world and what is all of that.
うん。人間であることを書くこと、世界で人間でいる意味、その全部。

236
00:41:52,125 --> 00:41:54,045
I think as someone who makes something, there's a version of sort of accepting you're not in control of it
ものを作る人として、コントロールしてない、って受け入れる方がある。

237
00:41:52,125 --> 00:41:54,045
like you're saying when it happens that I think can be really terrifying to be like, "Oh, I don't control - Anything." Ah, right. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
君が言う、それが起きるとき。何もコントロールしてない。ああ、うん、うん。

238
00:41:54,045 --> 00:42:02,865
- But the other side of that is actually, it can be really liberating to be like, "Oh, I'm not in control of this." You can kind of let go.
反対側は、実際すごく楽になる。これをコントロールしてない。手放せる。

239
00:42:02,865 --> 00:42:24,745
And I wonder if part of that is - - Yeah, yeah. No, fair enough. You need to retain a healthy skepticism about all of it.
その一部は、と思う。うん、うん。公平だ。全部に、健全な疑いを残した方がいい。

240
00:42:25,665 --> 00:42:30,145
But then I also think I would never cast it in a kind of like I'm trying to talk about the human experience at all. I would never think of it like that.
でも、人間の経験について話そうとしてる、みたいには絶対思わない。そうは枠づけない。

241
00:42:30,145 --> 00:42:45,645
I think that's kind of like the art school side of me.
それは、僕の美大の側みたいだ。

242
00:42:45,645 --> 00:42:48,725
By the time you've done three years of art school and you've gone through the process of explaining and watching other people trying to explain their work and get trashed for your work and
美大を3年やって、説明する過程、他の人が仕事を説明しようとするのを見て、壊されて、

243
00:42:45,645 --> 00:43:02,225
all this kind of... You end up thinking that the process is the point. And that's the kind, I think me and Stanny went to the same art school and we both came out with the process being the point.
そういう全部。過程が本体だ、って思うようになる。僕とスタンリーは同じ美大で、二人とも過程が本体だ、って出てきた。

244
00:43:02,225 --> 00:43:17,165
Yes, there's an end result. The real juice is the process.
うん、結果はある。本当の汁は過程だ。

245
00:43:17,165 --> 00:43:19,705
The real juice is like, "I don't know what I'm doing, but in finding the things that speak to me and assembling them and presenting them and then moving on, that is my act.
本当の汁は、何をしてるか分からない。でも自分に話しかけるものを見つけて、組み立てて、出して、先に進む。それが僕の行為。

246
00:43:21,345 --> 00:43:30,145
What you see and whether it speaks to you or whether I'm looking for that because I need to say something is like none of those things are linear.
君が見るもの、君に届くか、何か言いたくてそれを探してるか。どれもまっすぐじゃない。

247
00:43:31,005 --> 00:43:46,325
And in order for good shit to happen, you need to believe in the process more than anything else."
いいものが起きるには、何より過程を信じる必要がある。

248
00:43:46,325 --> 00:43:53,245
Kind of makes me think of some of my favorite records, you can almost hear that in the records in the way that they've been made. They were just enjoying putting this stuff together and they didn't care.
好きなレコードのいくつかを思い出す。作り方に、それが聴こえる。これを組み立てるのを楽しんでて、気にしてなかった。

249
00:43:53,245 --> 00:44:00,586
Same thing as doing for lyrics and ending up using the lyrics that just mean something to you and you don't know why, right?
歌詞と同じ。自分に意味があって、なぜか分からない行を使うことになる。

250
00:44:00,586 --> 00:44:23,406
The more you do it, the more liberated you become from the end result and the more liberated you become, the more you see if you're lucky
やるほど、結果から自由になる。自由になるほど、運がよければ見える。

251
00:44:20,586 --> 00:44:29,186
the fact that what you do might speak to people as, yeah, you're not responsible for that In a good way. Not like, oh, I don't care because you do care, but you just want to move on.
やってることが人に届くかもしれない、ってこと。いい意味で、それに責任はない。気にしない、じゃない。気にはする。ただ先に進みたい。

252
00:44:29,186 --> 00:44:43,166
I mean, the worst thing for me about OkComputer was when we finished that record, we'd been already playing those songs too much. And then when the manager said to us, "Now you're going to go on two years."
『OK Computer』でいちばん悪かったのは、終わったとき、もうあの曲を弾きすぎてたこと。マネージャーが、これから2年行く、って。

253
00:44:43,166 --> 00:44:56,126
I'm like, I literally begged them, "I can't do it. I can't do it. I need to move on." Two years later, we were still going.
文字通り頼んだ。できない。できない。先に進まなきゃ。2年後も、まだ続いてた。

254
00:44:57,146 --> 00:45:05,106
And that was just really fucked up because the moving on thing is like, yeah, that's not a luxury. That's entirely essential. I think everybody has their different ways of dealing with this.
本当にひどかった。先に進むのは、贅沢じゃない。どうしても必要だ。みんな、やり方は違うと思う。

255
00:45:06,686 --> 00:45:22,106
Personally, I rely on the sort of thing of not doing it for too long.
個人的には、長くやりすぎないことに頼ってる。

256
00:45:22,106 --> 00:45:23,426
I wish I could do it for a long time, but I can't because it sort of starts to get disconnected and a bit like, oh, I've got this move here and we do
長くできたらよかった。でもできない。切れて、ここでこの動き、ここでこの部分、になって、その場の感じがなくなる。

257
00:45:22,106 --> 00:45:34,086
this bit here and it doesn't feel very spontaneous. And the whole point of a live performance is rediscovering with the people you're standing in front of something about this thing that you wrote and sharing it with them.
ライブの要点は、目の前の人と、書いたものについて何かを見つけ直して、分かち合うこと。

258
00:45:34,086 --> 00:45:43,866
So that demands that you sort of... As much as a spectator as a performer, maybe that's the wrong way of putting it, but I don't think you...
だから、それを求める。見てる側でもあって、やってる側でもある。言い方が違うかも。でも。

259
00:45:43,866 --> 00:45:53,926
I used to, but now I don't think you need to dig deep in order to find what you need to sing this song or whatever. I think it's the opposite.
昔はそう思ってた。今は、この曲を歌うために深く掘る必要はないと思う。逆だ。

260
00:45:53,926 --> 00:46:02,306
You need to just totally let go and go, "If I'm not feeling this right now I'm good with that because maybe in 10 minutes it's going to really hit me like a train," which often it does.
完全に手放して、今これを感じてなくても、それでいい。10分後に電車みたいに来るかもしれない。よくそうなる。

261
00:46:02,306 --> 00:46:23,327
- You have so many moments in your life, I think everyone does, where you have those songs, if you like music at all, where
人生には、そういう瞬間がすごく多い。音楽が好きなら、みんなそうだと思う。

262
00:46:19,907 --> 00:46:31,267
you're like, "Oh, this is also a song or this is also a movie or something." I remember seeing Pulp Fiction for the first time and being like, "Oh, this is a film as well."
あ、これも曲なんだ、とか、これも映画なんだ。『パルプ・フィクション』を初めて見たとき、あ、これも映画なんだ、って。

263
00:46:31,267 --> 00:46:39,227
And hearing Shine on You Crazy Diamond for the first time and being so confused by it that this was a song and that was what you could do also.
『Shine On You Crazy Diamond』を初めて聴いて、これが曲で、こういうこともできるんだ、って混乱した。

264
00:46:40,407 --> 00:47:02,147
With the Carmen Police video, for example, I remember watching that and being like, "Oh, this is so brilliant." I feel like so much of your music has that weight where it really, you just remember hearing it.
『Karma Police』のビデオも、見て、これはすごい、って。君の音楽の多くには、その重さがある。聴いたことを本当に覚えてる。

265
00:47:02,147 --> 00:47:16,960
It's all so different from each other in a way that is, I think, so inspiring just to see someone be so consistently great in so many different ways, but
互いにとても違って、それが刺激になる。こんなに違う形で、こんなにずっとすごい人を見ること。

266
00:47:15,247 --> 00:47:29,147
in so many different ways. It can be hard to timeline it sometimes in terms of... There's not this obvious like, "Well, it was this, then it was this, then it was this, then it was this."
こんなに違う形で。順番にするのが難しいときがある。これがこう、それからこれ、が明らかじゃない。

267
00:47:30,687 --> 00:47:47,087
It's all its own thing in a way that I think just makes it feel so fresh always. I've just always really been drawn to stuff like... I love Codex or True Love White or something.
全部がそれ自身で、いつも新しく感じる。ずっと惹かれてきた。『Codex』とか『True Love Waits』とかが好き。

268
00:47:43,467 --> 00:48:06,187
Yeah. I just have always loved that sort of... I mean, everything in its right place is it for me. It's always been so much of that. I mean, I find it so inspiring.
うん。ずっとあの感じが好き。『Everything in Its Right Place』は、僕にとってはそれ。ずっとそう。すごく刺激になる。

269
00:48:07,067 --> 00:48:19,187
- Everything's right place was my response to not being out right. We had this empty house, we had a grand piano, we had a mattress. That's the first thing I did when we moved into this house.
あれは、外に出てないことへの返事だった。空き家があって、グランドピアノがあって、マットレスがあった。この家に引っ越して最初にやったこと。

270
00:48:19,187 --> 00:48:29,708
I mean, finding lost, felt lost ever since that okay can be a thing. And when I found that, okay, weirdly with that one, it was like a mantra.
迷子を見つけて、あれ以来、大丈夫であり得る、って感じた。あれは、奇妙に、唱える言葉みたいだった。

271
00:48:33,028 --> 00:48:44,128
I wasn't thinking, oh, I'm writing a song. I was just really enjoying playing piano in this empty house. And I had one CD, which was a and that's it.
曲を書いてる、とは思ってなかった。空き家でピアノを弾くのを、ただ楽しんでた。CDは一枚だけ。それだけ。

272
00:48:44,128 --> 00:48:55,208
And it was during the winter and there was storms all the time and just had a mattress. It was so great. Just playing this riff over and over and over again to make myself feel better.
冬で、いつも嵐で、マットレスだけ。最高だった。気分をよくするために、このリフを何度も何度も。

273
00:48:57,088 --> 00:49:10,588
I love that thing of, it arrives in the post, you plug it in, you don't read the manual, you just start messing about and you find something
郵便で届いて、挿して、説明書は読まない。いじり始めて、何かを見つける、あの感じが好き。

274
00:49:08,388 --> 00:49:18,348
whether it's a synth or a drum machine or guitar or anything.
シンセでも、ドラムマシンでも、ギターでも。

275
00:49:18,348 --> 00:49:20,708
Establishing a relationship with this construction, this thing and responding to it and responding to what people thought you might like about it, what you don't like about it, all this stuff.
この機械、このものと関係を作る。反応する。人が好きかもしれないと思うものにも、嫌いなものにも。全部。

276
00:49:20,708 --> 00:49:35,768
I've got so much out of that kind of process over the years that I figured that it must be part of what writing is, part of what our particular process is.
何年も、あの過程からすごく多くを得た。書くことの一部、僕らのやり方の一部なんだ、って分かった。

277
00:49:35,768 --> 00:49:46,888
There are just some days when nothing happened and then some days you'll pick up something you're unfamiliar with and the unfamiliarity means you have to make a sort of step
何も起きない日もある。知らないものを拾う日もある。知らないから、一歩を踏み出さなきゃいけない。

278
00:49:42,908 --> 00:50:05,168
you have to step across. And that stepping across means like, okay, I don't know why I like what's happening here and the I don't know why I like thing is what music should be, which
向こう側に出なきゃ。その一歩は、ここで起きてることがなぜ好きか分からない。なぜ好きか分からない、が音楽であるべきこと。

279
00:50:03,088 --> 00:50:20,748
is why strangely enough, algorithms don't produce an awful lot of that.
だから奇妙に、アルゴリズムはそれをあまり生まない。

280
00:50:20,748 --> 00:50:21,908
The human creativity is kind of like the same kind of thing as a scientist trying to solve a problem and he has all the things set up and he's gone
人間が作ることは、問題を解こうとする科学者と同じ種類だ。全部並べて、

281
00:50:20,748 --> 00:50:29,988
he's tried everything and he can't figure it out. He's in the same kind of place, right? He's in the like, where I'm lost now, I've tried everything.
全部試して、分からない。同じ場所にいる。今は迷子だ。全部試した。

282
00:50:29,988 --> 00:50:37,848
And then he goes away and he sleeps on it and he comes back and then something clicks. Because why?
それから離れて、寝て、戻ってきて、何かがカチッとなる。なぜ？

283
00:50:37,848 --> 00:50:56,289
Because he's unfamiliar, because he can't make sense or something and then he can or she suddenly makes sense, something falls into place.
知らないから。意味が作れないから。それから急に意味が通る。何かがはまる。

284
00:50:56,289 --> 00:51:07,189
- For me, it's just kind of where the risk falls is kind of inverted on itself of where you think it might be.
僕にとっては、リスクが落ちる場所が、思う場所と逆になってる。

285
00:51:07,189 --> 00:51:11,409
Someone else might feel like trying something different feels really risky in some way or straying from what people like about your music feels like a risky thing.
違うことを試すのが怖い人もいる。人が好きなことから外れるのが怖い。

286
00:51:11,409 --> 00:51:25,889
But I think the trying to recreate for that reason is that's the risky thing. - That's just awful. - That to me is the terrifying part, the idea of doing that.
でもその理由で同じものを作り直すのが、怖いことだと思う。それはただ最悪。僕にとって怖いのは、それをする考え。

287
00:51:25,889 --> 00:51:36,589
And I think any time... Sometimes there's songs that we've done in the studio and you finish it and you're like, "I think this is a great song.
スタジオで曲を終えて、これはいい曲だと思う。

288
00:51:37,609 --> 00:51:48,749
I think it's really good." But it doesn't excite me at all. Maybe because it has something that reminds you of something else you've done or whatever it is that it's just - I should like this.
本当にいい。でも全然興奮しない。他にやった何かを思い出させるから、とか。ただ、これは好きであるべきだ。

289
00:51:48,749 --> 00:51:57,969
You're not learning anything. I should - Like this. Yeah. I - Should like this. I should like it, but you just don't. And - - I mean, I had that with Codex all the way through it.
何も学んでない。これは好きであるべき。うん。好きであるべき。好きであるべきなのに、ただ好きじゃない。『Codex』はずっとそれだった。

290
00:51:57,969 --> 00:52:23,529
The rest of the band forced me personally to finish it. Everyone was like, "You're fucking crazy, man." I'm like, "All right. So sometimes..."
バンドの残りが、僕に仕上げさせた。みんな、狂ってるぞ、って。分かった。ときどき。

291
00:52:23,529 --> 00:52:24,909
- For me, at least so much of the studio experience of making something is almost trying to block out the fact that anyone's going to listen to it for a second to just
僕にとっては、何かを作るスタジオの多くは、誰かが聴く、って事実を一瞬でも閉め出そうとすること。

292
00:52:23,529 --> 00:52:30,649
make what - It is. You're doing it for you. - Yeah. You have to just be making what do I want to listen to at this time in my life?
それであるものを作る。自分のためにやってる。うん。今の人生で、自分が聴きたいものを作らなきゃ。

293
00:52:30,649 --> 00:52:40,689
What is it that I feel like I want to do, say, make? And the fact that people are going to hear it feels like that happens late.
やりたい、言いたい、作りたいと感じるものは何？ 人が聴く、って事実は、あとで起きる感じ。

294
00:52:40,689 --> 00:52:57,369
The realization of that feels like it happens later. And I think the live playing music to an audience is the part where you're embracing the fact that people listen to it and that it's connected with people.
その気づきは、あと。ライブで人にやるのは、人が聴いて、人とつながった、ってことを受け入れる部分。

295
00:52:57,369 --> 00:53:17,110
And I think for me, the kind of emotion that happened at the end of the London shows was like...
ロンドンのショーの終わりに来た感情は、僕には。

296
00:53:17,110 --> 00:53:25,530
I think it was just a realization of that those shows felt so much to me like a culmination of so many people's support, I think. I think that's the bit.
すごく多くの人の支えが、一つに集まったみたいに感じた。そこだと思う。

297
00:53:21,730 --> 00:53:34,370
That part feels so external. I think you make the thing, and then how many shows you play is determined by how many people want to come see it.
その部分は、すごく外にある。ものを作る。何本やるかは、見に来たい人の数で決まる。

298
00:53:35,530 --> 00:54:01,790
And I think what - Was - Turns out a - Lot. What was emotional for me was it felt... I was kind of realizing, I think, how deep it is and how deeper it is
結果、たくさんだった。僕にとって感情的だったのは、気づいてたんだと思う。どれだけ深いか。

299
00:53:57,370 --> 00:54:14,190
how much deeper it is than kind of any one moment or one song or any sort of fluke thing.
一つの瞬間や、一曲や、まぐれより、どれだけ深いか。

300
00:54:14,190 --> 00:54:18,510
It just felt like supported kind of in a way more than like, "We're here because we want to hear this song that you've done
支えられてる感じだった。この曲を聴きたいから来た、より、

301
00:54:14,190 --> 00:54:20,050
or we're here because we like when you used to play that one, or it just felt like we're here because we believe in what you're doing." And I think that's what it felt like to me.
昔あの曲をやってたのが好きだから、より、やってることを信じてるから来てる、感じ。僕にはそう感じた。

302
00:54:20,050 --> 00:54:35,010
And I think that's why I found it so emotional was I think... - Also, you have to give a lot of yourself. You have to give a lot of yourself, and that makes you very open.
だから感情的だった。あと、自分をたくさん渡さなきゃいけない。たくさん渡す。それでとても開く。

303
00:54:36,130 --> 00:54:48,750
I've heard people say to me, "Careful with that, because you're exposed to all these people and all this kind of thing." And like, "Yeah, it doesn't bother me.
気をつけろ、こんな人たち、こんなものに晒される、って言われた。うん、気にならない。

304
00:54:48,750 --> 00:55:11,650
Did, it did. It did. Used to. It doesn't bother me anymore." - I mean, I think if you're sensitive enough to want to write, sensitive enough to be aware of the part of you that wants to say anything
昔は気になった。昔は。もう気にならない。書くほど敏感なら、何かを言いたい部分に気づくほど敏感なら、

305
00:55:09,950 --> 00:55:19,070
if you're sensitive enough to care enough about a song to be like, "It's not right yet.
曲を気にするほど敏感なら、まだ正しくない、って。

306
00:55:20,170 --> 00:55:21,490
I have this idea and I want it to be right and I want it to be great," there's a sensitivity there that needs it to be great.
このアイデアがあって、正しくしたい。すごくしたい。そこには、すごくある必要がある繊細さがある。

307
00:55:21,490 --> 00:55:45,731
And I think if you're sensitive enough to do that, to feel it and then to communicate it and then see it through put so much of your life into making sure this synth sound is correct
それを感じるほど敏感なら、伝えて、最後まで見て、このシンセの音が正しいように人生の多くを入れるなら、

308
00:55:43,591 --> 00:56:03,571
then I think you're sensitive enough to feel when those moments land on you in what might come back at you.
その瞬間が自分に着地するとき、返ってくるものを感じるほど、敏感だと思う。

309
00:56:03,571 --> 00:56:05,211
I think a lot of the time when you're making stuff, you're kind of trying to reject what's coming back at you to remain sort of beginner's mind of how do I go back
ものを作ってるとき、多くは、返ってくるものを拒もうとしてる。初めての気持ちを残すために。

310
00:56:03,571 --> 00:56:23,771
to before anyone liked this song that I did or anything like that. You're always trying to get back to a sense of starting again.
この曲が好きになる前に戻るには、って。いつも、また始める感じに戻ろうとしてる。

311
00:56:23,771 --> 00:56:26,231
So I think those moments where you kind of let go and open up and allow it all to come in and you get to feel that
だから手放して、開いて、全部を入れさせて、それを感じる瞬間は、

312
00:56:23,771 --> 00:56:37,611
I think it is a wave of something that you don't live with every day. So it's quite - Like - You certainly don't, bro.
毎日一緒にいない波だと思う。だからかなり。君は確かに、そうじゃないよ。

313
00:56:39,951 --> 00:56:41,271
I guess if I was going to answer that I'd say when we did the first radio show in Madrid, we had to do this thing where we had to walk through the audience.
それに答えるなら、マドリードで最初のラジオのショーをしたとき、客の中を歩かなきゃいけなかった。

314
00:56:41,271 --> 00:56:56,200
Never done that before. Walk through the audience and we had to stand on the stage getting prepped before we went up, up.
やったことがなかった。客の中を歩いて、上がる前にステージで準備した。

315
00:56:56,200 --> 00:57:02,691
The first time we did that after, I don't know how many years, all of us, the whole band were like, "Okay, keep it together. Keep it together."
何年かぶりにそれをやったあと、全員が、大丈夫、持ちこたえて、って。

316
00:56:59,991 --> 00:57:16,271
Because I just wanted to... I already wanted to burst into tears because I hadn't been exposed to whatever that was for so long and to walk through the middle of it and then I never forget it.
もう泣き出したかった。あんなに長く、あれに触れてなかった。真ん中を歩いて、忘れない。

317
00:57:16,271 --> 00:57:23,971
I mean, all of us, we were like, "Holy shit. Holy shit." Also, you mentioned Beginner's Mind. Do you know the book?
全員、なんてことだ、なんてことだ、って。初心の心にも触れた。あの本、知ってる？

318
00:57:23,971 --> 00:57:36,451
- Yeah. - The Suzuki book. Okay, good. Very important. - Very important. Which brings us back to walking the artist's perspective where we began. There's the bow, there's the seal.
うん。鈴木の本。分かった、いい。すごく大事。すごく大事。それで、始めたところに戻る。歩くこと、アーティストの視点。お辞儀があって、締めがある。

